;; A vector literal is the array it looks like.
;;
;; `[1 2 3]` used to open an `RrbBuilder`, `Add` each element as a statement of
;; its own, and call `ToImmutable` — which also made the literal statement-shaped,
;; so it could not appear where an expression was wanted without being hoisted to
;; a local first. It is now one expression: an array, handed to `FromArray`,
;; which chunks straight into leaves.
;;
;; No behavioural test can see the difference — the vector is the same vector —
;; which is why the assertion is on the emitted C#.
;;
;; Since literal hoisting landed, two of the three assertions below match text
;; that has *moved*: a vec of literals is now built once into a static field of
;; `__Literals` rather than in the method, so the `FromArray` call they look for
;; is the field's initializer. The shape being asserted is unchanged and that is
;; what this file is for — one array, handed over whole, no `Add` — but if you
;; are reading this to find out where `three` builds its vector, the answer is
;; that it no longer does. See `literal_hoisting.bjo`. The third assertion, the
;; one with a hoisted element in it, is untouched: an element that needs
;; statements is not a literal, so that vec is still built where it is written.
(import (std prelude))

;; The whole of it on one line, `Add`-free.
;; EXPECT-CS: Collections\.RrbBuilder<int>\.FromArray\(new int\[\] \{ 1, 2, 3 \}, true\)
(: three (-> (Vec int)))
(defun (three) [1 2 3])

;; Empty is the same shape rather than a special case: `FromArray` answers
;; `Empty` for a zero-length array.
;; EXPECT-CS: Collections\.RrbBuilder<string>\.FromArray\(new string\[\] \{  \}, true\)
(: none (-> (Vec string)))
(defun (none) [])

;; An element that needs statements of its own is still hoisted ahead of the
;; literal, exactly as an argument to any other call is. `__hoist` is what
;; `prepareOperands` names one, and the literal names it rather than inlining a
;; `let` into an array initializer, which C# has no way to express.
;; EXPECT-CS: Collections\.RrbBuilder<int>\.FromArray\(new int\[\] \{ __hoist__[0-9]+, [0-9]+ \}, true\)
(: hoisting (-> (Vec int)))
(defun (hoisting) [(let ((t 5)) (* t 2)) 7])
